# Shell scripts keep LF endings, whatever core.autocrlf says.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to autocrlf=true, which rewrites these to CRLF on
# checkout. A shebang whose line ends in a carriage return is a script that
# dies on its very first line - "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" -
# before it can print anything useful. That is a bad way for `install.sh` to
# meet somebody on a machine they are setting up, and this folder is handed
# over as often on macOS as on Windows.
#
# The .bat files are the other way round on purpose and are left alone:
# cmd.exe expects CRLF.
*.sh  text eol=lf

# .command files are macOS shell scripts that run when double-clicked in
# Finder, and they need exactly the same treatment as the .sh files - they are
# the same thing with a different extension. Left out of the line above, they
# arrive on a Mac with CRLF endings and die on the shebang, before printing
# anything: "bad interpreter: /bin/bash^M". The customer sees a Terminal window
# flash and vanish, which says nothing about what went wrong.
#
# This is the file that made the first .command files possible to ship: without
# it the failure only appears on the machine that downloaded the .dmg, which is
# the one machine nobody can look at.
*.command  text eol=lf
